// Variant: Null-Fives & Dark Orange (6 Suits). 3 players, our_player_index=0.

// At pace 0 Alice must not throw a card that advances nothing.
//
// Replay 1973996 T52, `Null-Fives & Dark Orange (6 Suits)`. will-bot69 is Alice
// with stacks [0,5,5,5,5,0], 7 cards left and 10 points still owed across three
// players -- pace 0. Seven clue tokens, and she is not locked.
//
// Section 4 of the General Clue Evaluation List is the branch for a forced
// clue, and its gate used to read "Alice is LOCKED or at 8 clues". Neither held
// here, so `rung_4` returned nullptr, `choose_clue` declined, and phase 2 threw
// order 44 -- a card that advanced nothing, on a turn where every remaining
// turn has to produce a play. DECISION_MAKING.md's section 4 now reads
// "locked, at 8 clues, **or at pace 0** and is forced to clue or pitch", and
// this is that arm.
//
// A note on the DARK clue, because the obvious reading of this position is
// wrong. It looks as though a dark colour clue to will-bot67 ought to be the
// stall here -- naming the inverted suit goes down the orange ladder rather
// than the play branch, so it cannot be misread as a colour stable play. But
// it is rejected, and rightly. His leftmost orange is the d4 on slot 2 with the
// dark stack on 0, so the ladder would be telling him to CHUCK a card that
// cannot reach its stack: a misplay. `stable_colour`'s giver-only reject
// (interpret_clue.cpp, "the receiver will chuck this card whatever we do")
// catches it, and `analyse_clues` drops it before any rung sees it. The
// critical d1 that both bots can see sits in yagami_black's hand, not in
// will-bot67's, so it does not make the clue safe.
//
// What this rung is responsible for is therefore narrower than "give that
// clue": it is that she clues AT ALL rather than throwing a card at pace 0.
